      "title": "Australia news live: near miss for Jetstar and Qatar Airways planes taxiing at Sydney airport",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/aug/09/bird-flu-updates-politics-reactions-sarah-hanson-young-insiders-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Meanwhile, expert says bird flu detected on Murray-Darling basin ‘should be ringing alarm bells’. Follow updates</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p><strong>Coalition demands NDIS changes focus on fraud, corruption</strong></p><p>Liberal frontbencher <strong>Tim Wilson</strong> also called on the government to focus more on fraud and corruption issues in the NDIS, as the Coalition and Labor negotiate on changes to the disability scheme.</p><p><em>We have people who are awarding either packages to people when they shouldn’t be used, we’re having people taking advantage of the system and we know this is being exposed.</em></p><p><em>And so as a consequence we’ve seen this massive growth continue to go on and we need to make sure that that’s the focus, not the targeting of the clients. You know, if you go out to voters in Australia, there are a lot of people who are on disability support packages who are very nervous about their future and it’s because of how this government has looked at reforms and they feel like they’re being targeted, not the fraudsters.</em></p><p><em>We’re not wowsers, we do believe in protecting children, particularly from harm.</em></p><p><em>Under the government’s proposed bill, it doesn’t limit the capacity to advertise to children and that’s something that I’m concerned about, making sure that we’ve got the measures that are consistent, appropriate, and also reflecting the changing nature of technology</em></p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/aug/09/bird-flu-updates-politics-reactions-sarah-hanson-young-insiders-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-08-09T00:35:37"
    },
    {
      "title": "Centrelink pursuing debts that are more than 40 years old despite agreeing to waive old arrears",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/aug/09/centrelink-pursuing-debts-that-are-more-than-40-years-old-despite-agreeing-to-waive-old-arrears",
      "description": "<p>Advocates and legal bodies want the debt to be made ‘out of bounds’ given how hard it is to determine its accuracy</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/aug/09/bird-flu-updates-politics-reactions-sarah-hanson-young-insiders-ntwnfb\">Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates</a></p></li><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Centrelink is pursuing $4.93bn in unpaid debts, with the oldest dating back more than 40 years, despite the government agreeing to implement a six-year statute of limitations on debt recovery.</p><p>A growing list of advocates, including peak legal body Economic Justice Australia, want the debts wiped, while Australia’s leading welfare expert has warned it is impossible to trust whether the huge amount of debt is accurate.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/aug/09/centrelink-pursuing-debts-that-are-more-than-40-years-old-despite-agreeing-to-waive-old-arrears\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-08-08T20:00:07"
    },
    {
      "title": "Hospitality recruitment app Supp calls gig-workers contractors. Some users say it leads to casual work without entitlements",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/aug/09/hospitality-recruitment-app-supp-gig-workers-contractors-casual-work-entitlements-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p><strong>Exclusive</strong>: Workers’ rights experts raise ‘alarm bells’ over app requiring ABNs for jobs they say lack worker autonomy</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/aug/09/bird-flu-updates-politics-reactions-sarah-hanson-young-insiders-ntwnfb\">Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates</a></p></li><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Concerns have been raised that workers using an increasingly popular Australian hospitality gig-work app may be missing out on entitlements such as superannuation by being hired as “independent contractors”, similar to delivery riders.</p><p>Launched in Melbourne in 2017, Supp describes itself as a “jobs marketplace”, where hospitality businesses can advertise available shifts to potential workers at an hourly rate, and where workers can secure ad-hoc employment. It was created by Kate and Cameron Reid, the owners of Lune Croissanterie, who maintain a financial stake in the company but are no longer on its board.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/aug/09/hospitality-recruitment-app-supp-gig-workers-contractors-casual-work-entitlements-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-08-08T20:00:06"
    }
